{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Summary\n\nThe in-process library read path (`rebar._reads`) has **no initialization guard**, so every\nread against a repo whose tracker directory does not exist returns an EMPTY result with no\nerror. The CLI read path guards this exact condition\n(`_engine_support/reads_cli.py:340-342` -> \"ticket system not initialized\", exit 1), and the\nlibrary WRITE path guards it too (`_store/event_prepare.py:99-102`), but the library/MCP READ\npath does not.\n\nBecause the MCP read tools delegate straight to the library (`_mcp_reads.py:295` ->\n`rebar.list_tickets`), an MCP client pointed at a host with a missing store is told\n\"there are no tickets\" instead of \"this store is not initialized\".\n\n## Impact (observed in production)\n\nThe deployed rebar MCP server at `https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/mcp` has no\n`/app/.tickets-tracker`. It reports \"Connected\" and answers:\n\n- `list_tickets` / `search` / `ready_tickets` -> `[]`   (silent)\n- `show_ticket <real alias>`                  -> \"ticket_not_found\"  (loud but WRONG cause)\n- `fsck`                                      -> \"ticket system not initialized (/app/.tickets-tracker not found)\"  (correct)\n\nAn agent following the repo's MCP-first tracker rule therefore sees an empty tracker,\nconcludes no ticket exists for its work, and files duplicates -- the precise\nsilent-data-hiding failure mode already recorded as a defect for the store-divergence case\nin bug bilingual-capitalist-caecilian (\"Silent data-hiding: no error is surfaced to the\noperator; the store simply looks empty\") and for the filter case in bug spiny-ferry-ripen.\n\n## Authoritative sources violated\n\n- `docs/mcp-reference.md:7` -- MCP faults are surfaced as distinct error codes \"instead of\n  silently resolving to a fallback\", so \"a driving agent can tell a broken config from a\n  deliberate policy refusal\". `[]` for a missing store defeats exactly this.\n- Surface parity: the CLI (`reads_cli.py:340`) and library writes (`event_prepare.py:99`)\n  both raise on this condition; library reads alone do not.\n\n## Root cause\n\n`_reads.py` resolves the tracker through `_tracker()` (`_reads.py:31-33`) and never checks\nthat the directory exists; all 8 read entries funnel through it unguarded.\n\nOrigin: commit 7d53bc5b43b7 (2026-06-09, \"#2 Steps 0/2/3\") introduced the in-process read\npath, stating it replicated \"each shim's exact contract\" while explicitly noting the\nin-process reads \"do not run the dispatcher's `_ensure_initialized`\". Its parity test\ncovered an *empty* store but never an *uninitialized* one; that test was later deleted as\n\"now-moot\" by 28406790c61e. Ticket: tend-tomb-doe (df9b-663a-98ed-4dc4), whose own scope was\n\"Map in-process exceptions to existing RebarError shapes\" + \"parity tests green both ways\".\n\n## Reproduction (purely local, no network)\n\n    tmp=$(mktemp -d); cd \"$tmp\"; git init -q .\n    python3 -c \"import rebar; print(rebar.list_tickets(repo_root='$tmp'))\"   # -> []      (silent)\n    python3 -c \"import rebar; rebar.create_ticket('task','x',repo_root='$tmp')\"  # -> RebarError: not initialized\n    rebar list                                                              # -> Error: not initialized, exit 1\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] A library read (`list_tickets`, `search`, `recent_session_logs`) against a repo with no\n      tracker directory raises the same not-initialized `RebarError` the write path raises,\n      instead of returning `[]`.\n- [x] `show_ticket` against an uninitialized store reports the store as uninitialized rather\n      than the misleading \"Ticket '<id>' not found\".\n- [x] The corresponding MCP read tools surface that fault as a machine-readable error\n      envelope, not an empty array.\n- [x] An INITIALIZED-but-empty store still returns `[]` with no error (no regression).\n- [x] Regression coverage asserts read/write parity on an uninitialized store.\n\n## Scope of the read/write agreement — the predicates DIFFER, deliberately\n\nEarlier wording in this plan claimed \"read/write parity\" without qualification. That overstates\nit, and plan review was right to block on the claim. Stated precisely:\n\nReads and writes now agree that an **ABSENT tracker directory** is an error. They deliberately\ndo NOT agree about a directory that EXISTS but is not a git repo:\n\n    write path   _store/event_prepare.py   isdir(tracker) AND exists(tracker/.git)\n    CLI reads    _engine_support/reads_cli.py:340,384   isdir(tracker) only\n    library reads  _reads._tracker (this change)        isdir(tracker) only\n\nThe read guard matches the CLI READ path, not the write path, and that is the correct target:\nthe defect was that library reads disagreed with CLI reads. Adopting the write predicate would\nmake library reads STRICTER than CLI reads — trading one asymmetry for a new one — and would\nbreak a live, supported case.\n\nVerified empirically on a tracker whose `.git` was removed (the shape a `git archive` of the\norphan `tickets` branch produces, documented in `tests/unit/test_store_copy_needs_env_id.py`):\n\n    tracker dir exists: True | .git present: False\n      READ  -> 1 ticket(s): reads WORK\n      WRITE -> refused: \"ticket system not initialized\"\n\nSo reads on a `.git`-less store are working behavior that real fixtures depend on, not an\noversight. The write path's extra `.git` check guards event APPENDS (provenance stamping) and\nhas no read-side analogue.\n\nThe regression test is named for what it actually asserts —\n`test_reads_and_writes_agree_that_an_absent_store_is_an_error` — i.e. the absent-directory case\nonly. No test claims the broader parity."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787727953182575001,"uuid":"ffb2f247-3ac0-456a-a978-f9f580e0c6fb"}